## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design both memoized and tabulated DP for a non-trivial partition constraint, reinforcing DP patterns and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Top-down memoization with arrays
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Power-of-two check with bit trick

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPartitions(nums){
  const n = nums.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+nums[i];
  const isPow2 = (x)=> x>0 && (x&(x-1))===0;
  // Memoized top-down
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(-1);
  const top = (i)=>{
    if(i===n) return 1;
    if(memo[i]!==-1) return memo[i];
    let res = 0;
    for(let j=i+1;j<=n;j++){
      const s = pref[j]-pref[i];
      if(isPow2(s)) res += top(j);
    }
    memo[i]=res; return res;
  };
  // Bottom-up
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[n]=1;
  for(let i=n-1;i>=0;i--){
    let sumWays=0;
    for(let j=i+1;j<=n;j++){
      const s = pref[j]-pref[i];
      if(isPow2(s)) sumWays += dp[j];
    }
    dp[i]=sumWays;
  }
  return {topWays: top(0), bottomWays: dp[0]};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for large n or multiple queries on the same array?
- How would you handle large counts and modulo arithmetic to prevent overflow?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of multiple shortest paths and second-best paths with counting, using a single Dijkstra variant. It blends graph traversal and path combinatorics under constraints typical at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Two-best paths per node; counting paths for each distance tier
- Dijkstra relaxation with tie-handling
- Modulo counting for combinatorial paths

## Code Example
```javascript
// Two-best Dijkstra (conceptual)
function secondShortest(graph, s, t, MOD){
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const n = graph.length;
  const dist1 = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const dist2 = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const cnt1 = Array(n).fill(0);
  const cnt2 = Array(n).fill(0);
  dist1[s] = 0; cnt1[s] = 1;
  const pq = new MinHeap();
  pq.push([0, s]);
  while(!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [d, u] = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist2[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of graph[u]){
      const nd = d + w;
      if (nd < dist1[v]){
        dist2[v] = dist1[v]; cnt2[v] = cnt1[v];
        dist1[v] = nd; cnt1[v] = cnt1[u];
        pq.push([nd, v]);
      } else if (nd === dist1[v]){
        cnt1[v] = (cnt1[v] + cnt1[u]) % MOD;
      } else if (nd > dist1[v] && nd < dist2[v]){
        dist2[v] = nd; cnt2[v] = cnt1[u];
        pq.push([nd, v]);
      } else if (nd === dist2[v]){
        cnt2[v] = (cnt2[v] + cnt1[u]) % MOD;
      }
    }
  }
  return [dist2[t], cnt2[t] % MOD];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to count distinct second-shortest paths that share subpaths?  
- How does this adapt if negative weights are allowed but no negative cycles exist?

